@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction
@cindex experimental features
  Tessera X mu4e prepares independent context snapshots from local mail.
The @samp{x} means experimental.  This version implements selection,
subthread, and today snapshots, using native mu4e records and the local
mu index.

  A snapshot is a read-only buffer containing selected message headers
and available body text.  The buffer from which you start preparation is
the @dfn{source buffer}; the selected messages form its @dfn{scope}.

  The commands work without @code{tessera-mu4e-mode}.  Preparing a
context does not synchronize mail or send a language-model request.  The
shared result format, size limits, and snapshot commands are described
in @ref{Top,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started

  The package uses mu4e's configured mail paths and mu executable.  Set
up mu4e and its local index before preparing snapshots.

@menu
* Requirements::        Required packages and loading libraries.
* First Context::       Preparing and viewing a snapshot.
@end menu

@node Requirements
@section Requirements
@cindex installation
  The declared minimum versions are Emacs 30.1, Tessera 0.1.0, Tessera X
0.1.0, and mu4e 1.14.3.  Tessera also requires Alert 1.2 or later.  For
a source checkout, add @file{lisp/tessera/}, @file{lisp/tessera-x/}, and
@file{lisp/tessera-x-mu4e/} to @code{load-path}, together with mu4e's
own library directory.  Then use:

@lisp
(require 'tessera-x-mu4e)
@end lisp

  Use @kbd{M-x customize-group @key{RET} tessera-mu4e @key{RET}} to
configure the options documented here.  You can also load
@code{tessera-mu4e} and set them in your initialization file before
loading the snapshot commands.

@node First Context
@section Preparing a First Context

  In a mu4e Headers buffer, move point to a message and run @kbd{M-x
tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-context}.  The command reads the selected local
message files.  Run @kbd{M-x tessera-x-show-context} in that Headers
buffer to inspect the successful snapshot.

  For expanded subthreads and today queries, completion can be
asynchronous.  The show command continues to show an older successful
snapshot while a replacement is pending.  @xref{Completion and
Cancellation}.

@node Selecting Messages
@chapter Selecting Messages

  These commands select messages in the Headers buffer without executing
pending marked operations or changing read state.

@menu
* Marks and Regions: Marked Messages and Regions.  Marks, the region, or message at point.
* Current Subthread::   The current message and its descendants.
@end menu

@node Marked Messages and Regions
@section Marked Messages and Regions
@cindex marks
@cindex region
@deffn Command tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-context
Prepare marked messages in the current Headers buffer; if there are no
marks, use rows intersecting the active region; otherwise use the
message at point.  Return a context object.  Signal a user error outside
Headers or if no message is selected.
@end deffn

  Marks take precedence over the region.  They identify records; the
command neither executes nor clears their pending operations.  Folded
rows remain eligible.  Each selected message is included only once.
Local bodies are read synchronously before the command returns.

@node Current Subthread
@section The Current Message and Its Replies
@cindex subthreads
@deffn Command tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-subthread-context &optional @
  local-index
Prepare the message at point and its descendants.  A non-@code{nil}
@var{local-index}, or an interactive prefix such as @kbd{C-u}, requests
supplementary records from the local mu index.  Return a context object.
Signal a user error outside Headers or when there is no current message.
@end deffn

  By default, only current results are examined, including folded rows.
A descendant is a reply to the current message, or a reply to one of its
replies.  When @code{mu4e-search-threads} is non-@code{nil}, Tessera
follows mu4e's thread structure together with reply references in
message headers.  With threading disabled, it uses those references
alone.  Matching subjects do not by themselves establish a relationship.

  The current message and its descendants are selected, not all
ancestors or siblings in its conversation.  Without expansion, body
reading and publication are synchronous.

@cindex local index expansion
@defopt tessera-x-mu4e-subthread-scope
The default subthread scope.  Initially @code{results}, it can instead
be @code{local-index} to supplement replies outside the current result
filter.  A non-@code{nil} command argument also requests expansion.  A
prefix does not toggle expansion off when this option enables it.
@end defopt

  Expansion searches the local mu index for messages related to the
message at point.  Its @samp{Message-ID} header supplies the identity
for that search; expansion fails if the identifier is missing.  Tessera
retains selected descendants already in the current results, adds
descendants found in the index, and removes duplicates.  The Headers
search remains unchanged.

  Unindexed mail or unavailable relatives can still be missing from the
snapshot.  Expansion does not fetch mail from a server.

@node Today Queries
@chapter Today Queries

  A today snapshot queries the local index within a base scope.  The
source buffer or a user-supplied query function determines that scope.

@menu
* Native Today Scopes::  Queries from Headers and Main buffers.
* Custom Today Scopes::  Supplying a base query function.
@end menu

@node Native Today Scopes
@section Native Today Scopes

@deffn Command tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-today-context
Obtain a base query from @code{tessera-x-mu4e-today-query-function},
combine it with @samp{date:today..now}, and start an asynchronous local
index request.  Return the context object.
@end deffn

  The default base-query function chooses the scope from the source
buffer:

@table @asis
@item Headers buffer
@cindex today, Headers query
Use the query associated with the buffer.  A missing query is a user
error.  This consults the index with the query, rather than limiting the
request to the number of rows currently displayed in Headers.

@item Main buffer
@cindex today, Main query item
Use the native bookmark or maildir query item on the current line.
Point on an unrelated line is a user error; the command does not
silently choose an account or query all mail.
@end table

  With a nonempty base query, the resulting expression is
@samp{(@var{base}) AND (date:today..now)}.  An empty base selects
@samp{date:today..now} alone.  The date range is interpreted by mu as
today through now; Tessera does not substitute a fixed 24-hour interval.

  No mailbox synchronization occurs.  An empty index result is a
successful empty snapshot and still invokes the ready hook.

@node Custom Today Scopes
@section Custom Today Scopes
@cindex custom query functions
@defopt tessera-x-mu4e-today-query-function
A no-argument function returning the base mu query string.  The default
function reads the current Headers query or Main query item.  The
function runs in the source buffer before the request is created.
@end defopt

  A custom function can supply an account-specific scope or support
another source buffer.  It must return a string; use the empty string
only when today's query should have no additional restriction.

  For example, the following user function restricts today to a maildir.
Replace the maildir with one present in the configured index.

@lisp
(require 'tessera-mu4e)

(defun my-tessera-today-query ()
  "Return the mail scope for today's context."
  "maildir:/personal/Inbox")

(setq tessera-x-mu4e-today-query-function
      #'my-tessera-today-query)
@end lisp

  Both backend options can be set globally or given buffer-local values.
They affect later requests; a running query already has its selected
scope.

@node Local Retrieval
@chapter Local Retrieval

  All bodies are read from local message files.  Index access and file
access can fail independently.

@menu
* Index Process::       Choosing the mu executable and index.
* Message Files::       Local bodies, metadata, and missing files.
@end menu

@node Index Process
@section The Index Process
@cindex mu find
  Queries use @code{mu4e-mu-binary}.  A non-@code{nil}
@code{mu4e-mu-home} is expanded and passed as @samp{--muhome}; otherwise
the executable uses its own default.  Results are sorted by date with
duplicates removed.  Subthread expansion also searches for related
messages.

  A query with no matches produces an empty snapshot.  If the query
fails or its output cannot be read, preparation fails and reports the
error.  The Headers query and result buffer remain unchanged.

@node Message Files
@section Message Files and Metadata
@cindex missing message files
  For each selected record, Tessera resolves its readable local message
path through mu4e and reads the complete raw message.  It extracts text
and metadata without displaying the message, altering flags, verifying
signatures, or decrypting encrypted content.

  A missing or unreadable file becomes a content note on that item.
Metadata remains available even when the index still refers to a file
that has moved or disappeared.  @xref{Mail Bodies,,, tessera-x, Tessera
X}.

@cindex message metadata
  Items include the full subject, date, names and addresses, maildir,
labels, tags, flags, Message-ID, and References.  Labels and tags are
merged without duplicates.  Native inferred parents are recorded
separately.  Reading raw mail can add or refine available header fields.

  Conversation grouping uses identities and references, retaining the
input order within each connected conversation.  Display truncation,
contact abbreviation, and hidden columns do not limit snapshot metadata.
Character budgets are applied later to the rendered body text.
@xref{Character Budgets,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

@node Completion and Cancellation
@chapter Completion and Cancellation
@cindex cancellation
  Selection and ordinary subthread requests read their files
synchronously.  Expanded subthreads and today scopes return while a
separate query can still be pending.  The ready hook runs once after a
successful snapshot is published, including for zero matches.

  Starting another request in the same source cancels its pending query.
Killing the source or changing major mode does the same.  Cancellation
stops the index query.  It does not discard earlier successful snapshots
or cancel preparation started from other source buffers.  Use @kbd{M-x
tessera-x-cancel-context} in the source for explicit cancellation.
@xref{Snapshot Lifecycle,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

  The package installs no key bindings.  Bindings and consumers belong
to user configuration.  For the ready hook and a consumer example, see
@ref{Consuming Snapshots,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

  Check the query, index configuration, and message files when
preparation fails or omits expected content.

@menu
* Retrieval Failures: Index and File Failures.  Diagnosing queries and unavailable files.
* Scope Errors: Scope Selection Errors.  Missing identifiers or query items.
* Reporting Bugs::      Information to include in a bug report.
@end menu

@node Index and File Failures
@section Index and File Failures

  For an index-process failure, check @code{mu4e-mu-binary},
@code{mu4e-mu-home}, and the recorded error.  A different mu home can
contain different messages even when the query string is identical.  A
file content note indicates a body-read problem rather than a query
failure.

@node Scope Selection Errors
@section Scope Selection Errors
@cindex missing Message-ID
  If subthread expansion reports a missing Message-ID, ordinary result
selection may still work, but a query by that identity cannot be issued.
If today reports no native query item, use a Headers buffer with a
query, a Main bookmark or maildir line, or a custom query function.

@node Reporting Bugs
@section Reporting Bugs

  For hook and snapshot-buffer problems, see @ref{Troubleshooting,,,
tessera-x, Tessera X}.  Report backend issues at
@url{https://github.com/brsvh/emacs-tessera/issues}, including Emacs,
mu4e and package versions, command, scope, and relevant error text.

